-- allium: 3
-- responses.allium
--
-- OpenAI Responses API: wire contract.
--
-- Scope: How Phoenix translates an llm/LlmRequest to the OpenAI
--        Responses API wire format, normalizes a Responses API response
--        back to an llm/LlmResponse, accumulates a streaming SSE
--        response, and classifies Responses API failures — including the
--        codex ChatGPT-backend quota-exhaustion path — into the
--        llm/LlmErrorKind taxonomy.
--
-- Includes: Endpoint resolution; the request-translation discriminant
--           contract — the load-bearing ResponsesContentPartTypeValid
--           invariant; the capability gap where Anthropic server blocks
--           are dropped; response normalization with cached-token
--           de-duplication; the ResponsesStreamAccumulator state machine
--           including the response.completed output-recovery fallback;
--           SSE error classification; codex-backend 429/503 parsing
--           (REQ-LLM-006a).
--
-- Excludes: The internal vocabulary and error taxonomy (see llm.allium).
--           The Anthropic Messages wire format (see anthropic.allium).
--           The plan-aware quota *message wording* (render_usage_limit_
--           message in error.rs) — this file governs the classification,
--           not the localized string. Model registry / discovery.
--
-- Why this file exists: a 2026-05 production incident. A read_image tool
--           result fed back to an OpenAI model produced HTTP 400:
--             "Invalid value: 'text'. Supported values are: 'input_text',
--              'input_image', 'output_text', ..."
--           The request translator emitted a function_call_output content
--           part with the Chat-Completions discriminant `text` instead of
--           the Responses API discriminant `input_text`. There was no
--           normative contract on the wire discriminant; the proptest in
--           place asserted the broken shape. ResponsesContentPartTypeValid
--           is that contract.
--
-- Contrast with anthropic.allium: the same internal tool_result-with-
--           images block lands on `input_text` / `input_image` here and
--           on `text` / `image` there. The discriminant is provider-
--           specific.
--
-- Normative companions:
--   specs/llm/requirements.md  — REQ-LLM-001, 002, 004, 005, 006, 006a, 009
--   specs/llm/design.md        — OpenAI provider, codex 429 parsing
--
-- Cross-references:
--   crates/phoenix-ide/src/llm/openai.rs — translate_to_responses_request,
--       normalize_responses_api_response, ResponsesStreamAccumulator,
--       classify_responses_error, parse_codex_error, resolve_endpoint
--   crates/phoenix-ide/src/llm/rate_limit.rs — QuotaDetails, header parse
--   crates/phoenix-ide/src/llm/error.rs      — from_http_status

use "./llm.allium" as llm

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- How the Responses endpoint URL was resolved. Same precedence shape as
-- the Anthropic provider: explicit override verbatim, else the public default.
enum ResponsesEndpointSource { base_url_override | provider_default }

-- Lifecycle of a streaming SSE accumulation. `accumulating` is the only
-- non-terminal state. `done` is reached by response.completed; `failed`
-- by an `error` event, response.failed, or response.incomplete.
enum ResponsesStreamStatus { accumulating | done | failed }
enum CodexWebSocketDisposition { full | incremental | http_fallback | backend_error }

-- Where a translated wire content part sits. Both surfaces draw from the
-- same accepted-discriminant set — that shared set is exactly what the
-- 2026-05 incident violated for `function_call_output`.
--   message_content       — the `content` array of an input message item
--   function_call_output  — the `output` array of a function_call_output
--                           item (a tool result fed back to the model)
enum ResponsesPartSurface { message_content | function_call_output }

-- Discriminant of an output item in a Responses API response.
--   message       — assistant text, carried as `output_text` parts
--   function_call — a tool call the model wants Phoenix to run
--   reasoning     — internal model thinking; intentionally discarded
--   other         — any unrecognised item type; ignored with a debug log
enum ResponsesOutputKind { message | function_call | reasoning | other }

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Value Types
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A raw Responses API response, summarized to the facts normalization
-- branches on. `cached_tokens` is OpenAI's input_tokens_details.
-- cached_tokens — a subset already counted inside raw_input_tokens.
--
-- `has_output_content` is true iff at least one content block resulted
-- (non-empty message text — an output_text or a refusal part — or a
-- function_call). It is false when every output item was discarded
-- (reasoning) or empty — the condition the billed-but-empty guard
-- pairs with raw_output_tokens.
value ResponsesRawResponse {
    status: String
    raw_input_tokens: Integer
    raw_output_tokens: Integer
    cached_tokens: Integer
    cache_write_tokens: Integer
    has_function_call: Boolean
    has_output_content: Boolean
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The resolved Responses endpoint for one request.
entity ResponsesEndpoint {
    source: ResponsesEndpointSource
}

-- A streaming SSE accumulation in progress against the Responses API.
-- Created when the streaming HTTP response returns success.
--
-- `recovered_from_completed` records that the output items were salvaged
-- from the response.completed terminal payload because no per-item
-- `response.output_item.done` events arrived — a real gateway behaviour
-- (observed 2026-05-11) that otherwise persisted an empty agent message.
--
-- `input_tokens` is the raw count OpenAI reports; it is inclusive of
-- `cached_tokens`. The cached subset is split out only at normalization.
entity ResponsesStream {
    status: ResponsesStreamStatus
    input_tokens: Integer
    output_tokens: Integer
    cached_tokens: Integer
    cache_write_tokens: Integer
    recovered_from_completed: Boolean

    output_items: ResponsesOutputItem with stream = this

    transitions status {
        accumulating -> done
        accumulating -> failed
        terminal: done
        terminal: failed
    }
}

-- One completed output item collected from the stream — either from a
-- `response.output_item.done` event or, in the fallback path, from the
-- `/response/output` array of the `response.completed` event.
entity ResponsesOutputItem {
    stream: ResponsesStream
    item_kind: ResponsesOutputKind
}

-- One content part emitted into a Responses API request — an entry in
-- either a message `content` array or a function_call_output `output`
-- array. `wire_type` is the serialized `type` discriminant.
--
-- This entity exists so ResponsesContentPartTypeValid can range over
-- every emitted part. Before the 2026-05 fix the function_call_output
-- path produced parts with wire_type `text` / `image_url`; the message
-- path correctly produced `input_text` / `input_image`. The two paths
-- now share one part type, so they cannot diverge again.
entity ResponsesWireContentPart {
    surface: ResponsesPartSurface
    wire_type: String
}

-- A cacheable message content block on a GPT-5.6-era direct platform request.
-- Function-call outputs are not members of this entity because OpenAI rejects
-- prompt_cache_breakpoint on that surface.
entity ResponsesCacheBreakpoint {
    wire_type: String
    explicit: Boolean
}

-- A capability gap: an Anthropic server-handled block (server_tool_use,
-- *_tool_result, mcp_*) reached the Responses translator, which has no
-- wire equivalent for it. Recorded — not silent — so a provider-switch
-- context loss is diagnosable. Carries the dropped block's kind.
entity ResponsesDroppedServerBlock {
    block_kind: llm/ContentKind
    logged: Boolean
}

-- A GPT-5.6 request routed through ChatGPT/Codex authentication uses the
-- Responses Lite capability type. Its shape cannot carry platform-only
-- top-level tools, top-level instructions, or explicit cache controls.
entity CodexResponsesLiteRequest {
    model: String
    additional_tools_is_first: Boolean
    developer_instructions_is_second: Boolean
    parallel_tool_calls: Boolean
    reasoning_context: String
    compatibility_header: Boolean
}

-- A continuation snapshot is isolated by PromptCacheKey in the shared model
-- service. `prefix` contains every prior client input item followed by the
-- exact output items returned by the server.
entity CodexWebSocketSession {
    cache_key: String
    response_id: String
    compatibility_fingerprint: String
    connection_identity: String
    prefix: String
    idle_for: Duration
    dirty: Boolean
}

entity CodexWebSocketAttempt {
    disposition: CodexWebSocketDisposition
    emitted_before_success: Boolean
    beta_header: String
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules — endpoint resolution
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Endpoint precedence (resolve_endpoint): an explicit base-url override
-- is used verbatim; otherwise the public API default is used. Phoenix never
-- treats a configured URL as a gateway root and never appends
-- `/openai/v1/responses`.
rule ResponsesEndpointResolved {
    when: ResolveResponsesEndpoint(override_url?)

    ensures:
        if override_url != null:
            ResponsesEndpoint.created(source: base_url_override)
        else:
            ResponsesEndpoint.created(source: provider_default)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules — request translation
------------------------------------------------------------

rule CodexWebSocketContinuationSelected {
    when: SendCodexResponses(model, cache_key, request)

    requires: starts_with(model, "gpt-5.6")

    ensures:
        if CodexWebSocketSession.exists(
            cache_key: cache_key,
            compatibility_fingerprint: fingerprint_without_input(request),
            prefix: strict_prefix_of(request.input)
        ):
            CodexWebSocketAttempt.created(
                disposition: incremental,
                emitted_before_success: true,
                beta_header: "responses_websockets=2026-02-06"
            )
        else:
            CodexWebSocketAttempt.created(
                disposition: full,
                emitted_before_success: true,
                beta_header: "responses_websockets=2026-02-06"
            )

    @guidance
        -- The incremental response.create carries only the input suffix and
        -- previous_response_id. The fingerprint is the exact serialized request
        -- with input removed, so model, instructions/tools/tool choice, reasoning,
        -- store/stream/include, service tier, cache key, text controls, tags, and
        -- every future non-input field participate automatically. A successful
        -- terminal event replaces the session prefix with full input plus exact
        -- server output. Auth/model/property/compaction or non-prefix changes do
        -- not reuse the old response id.
        -- Text deltas and codex.rate_limits quota snapshots are emitted through
        -- the bounded single-consumer channel as their frames arrive. Only a
        -- non-empty text delta starts public output; quota frames leave the
        -- pre-output HTTP fallback window open.
}

rule CodexWebSocketFailureFallsBackOnce {
    when: CodexWebSocketAttemptFails(cache_key, failure_kind)

    ensures:
        CodexWebSocketSession.deleted(cache_key: cache_key)
        if failure_kind in { connect, transport, protocol, frame_timeout }:
            CodexWebSocketAttempt.created(
                disposition: http_fallback,
                emitted_before_success: true,
                beta_header: "responses_websockets=2026-02-06"
            )
        else:
            CodexWebSocketAttempt.created(
                disposition: backend_error,
                emitted_before_success: true,
                beta_header: "responses_websockets=2026-02-06"
            )

    @guidance
        -- Before any text is public, a transport/protocol failure issues exactly
        -- one full HTTP/SSE request. After text is public, transport failure is an
        -- interrupted network error and never replays the request. Backend/model
        -- errors return directly and never affect transport cooldown.
        -- Connect plus protocol handshake and each frame read have explicit
        -- terminal timeouts, so a half-open peer cannot retain the cohort lock.
        -- Each transport failure increments a shared Codex endpoint-pool
        -- exponential capped cooldown, so different prompt-cache cohorts also
        -- skip WebSocket. Expiry permits a retry and terminal WebSocket success
        -- resets the shared history.
}

rule CodexWebSocketPoolBoundedAndCancellationSafe {
    when: AcquireCodexWebSocket(cache_key, connection_headers)

    ensures:
        -- The connection identity is a one-way hash over the bearer credential
        -- and canonical case-insensitive connection-affecting custom headers,
        -- including chatgpt-account-id. Raw credentials are never retained.
        -- OpenAI-Beta is fixed to the WebSocket capability value; other custom
        -- headers survive the handshake.
        -- Idle sessions are evicted and sockets dropped; at capacity, the
        -- least-recently-used idle session is evicted before insertion.
        -- An attempt marks its entry dirty synchronously before socket use.
        -- Cancellation leaves it dirty; the next lock holder drops the socket
        -- before reuse. Terminal success or handled failure clears the marker.
        true
}

rule CodexResponsesLitePrefixTranslated {
    when: TranslateCodexResponsesRequest(model, request)

    requires: starts_with(model, "gpt-5.6")

    ensures:
        CodexResponsesLiteRequest.created(
            model: model,
            additional_tools_is_first: true,
            developer_instructions_is_second: true,
            parallel_tool_calls: false,
            reasoning_context: "all_turns",
            compatibility_header: true
        )

    @guidance
        -- The leading additional_tools item has role developer and contains
        -- the function tools in stable request order. The following developer
        -- message contains base instructions as one input_text part. Top-level
        -- instructions and tools are absent. The request retains store=false
        -- and prompt_cache_key. Direct-platform requests and pre-GPT-5.6 Codex
        -- requests retain the platform-shaped translation.
        -- Every WebSocket response.create also carries the Responses Lite
        -- selector in client_metadata; the upgrade header alone is insufficient
        -- because one connection carries multiple independent creates.
}

-- A message carrying at least one image is translated to a Responses API
-- message item whose `content` is an array of parts: one input_text part
-- per text block, one input_image part per image block. A message with
-- no images collapses to a plain-string `content` and emits no parts.
rule ResponsesMessageContentTranslated {
    when: TranslateResponsesMessage(message)

    requires: message.content.any(b => b.kind = image)

    ensures:
        for block in message.content:
            if block.kind = text:
                ResponsesWireContentPart.created(
                    surface: message_content, wire_type: "input_text"
                )
            if block.kind = image:
                ResponsesWireContentPart.created(
                    surface: message_content, wire_type: "input_image"
                )

    @guidance
        -- translate_to_responses_request, the text+image grouping branch.
        -- An input_image part's `image_url` is a bare data: URL string
        -- (data:{media_type};base64,{data}) — NOT a nested { url: ... }
        -- object. The nested form is the Chat Completions shape and the
        -- Responses API rejects it. tool_use and tool_result blocks in
        -- the same message are handled by separate function_call and
        -- function_call_output items, not by this rule.
}

-- LOAD-BEARING — the regression locus. A tool_result carrying images is
-- translated to a function_call_output item whose `output` is an array:
-- one input_text part, then one input_image part per image. A tool_result
-- with no images collapses to a plain-string `output` and emits no parts.
--
-- The discriminants MUST be input_text / input_image. The 2026-05
-- incident emitted `text` / `image_url` here — the Chat Completions
-- shape — and every OpenAI conversation that fed an image back through a
-- tool result became unrecoverable (the bad item stays in history, so a
-- retry re-sends it and re-fails).
rule ResponsesToolResultOutputTranslated {
    when: TranslateResponsesToolResult(block)

    requires: block.kind = tool_result
    requires: block.tool_result_images.count > 0

    ensures:
        ResponsesWireContentPart.created(
            surface: function_call_output, wire_type: "input_text"
        )
        for _ in block.tool_result_images:
            ResponsesWireContentPart.created(
                surface: function_call_output, wire_type: "input_image"
            )

    @guidance
        -- A function_call_output payload is model *input*, so its parts
        -- use the input_* discriminants — the same ones a user message's
        -- content array uses. The message path and the tool-result path
        -- emit the identical ResponsesWireContentPart type precisely so
        -- a future edit cannot make one correct and the other wrong.
        -- The text-only tool_result takes a separate string-output
        -- branch and creates no parts (the second requires clause).
}

rule ResponsesExplicitCacheBreakpointsPlaced {
    when: TranslateResponsesRequest(model_supports_explicit_cache, uses_codex_backend)

    requires: model_supports_explicit_cache = true
    requires: uses_codex_backend = false

    ensures: ResponsesCachePolicyEmitted(mode: "implicit", ttl: "30m")
    ensures: ResponsesCacheBreakpoints.count <= 50
    ensures:
        for breakpoint in ResponsesCacheBreakpoints:
            breakpoint.wire_type in { "input_text", "input_image", "input_file" }
            breakpoint.explicit = true

    @guidance
        -- Implicit mode owns the latest-message boundary, so Phoenix skips
        -- that message and preserves explicit markers on up to the latest 50
        -- earlier message boundaries considered for reads. OpenAI decides
        -- which newest boundaries consume its four write slots. The translator
        -- never traverses function_call_output items when placing markers.
        -- GPT-5.5-era and Codex bridge requests omit the policy and markers.
}

-- Capability gap. An Anthropic server-handled block has no Responses API
-- wire equivalent and is dropped from the request. The drop is recorded
-- and logged at debug with the block kind — a silent omission would be
-- indistinguishable from a translator bug.
rule ResponsesServerBlockDropped {
    when: TranslateResponsesBlock(block)

    requires: block.kind in {
        server_tool_use, mcp_tool_use,
        tool_search_tool_result, web_search_tool_result, web_fetch_tool_result,
        code_execution_tool_result, bash_code_execution_tool_result,
        text_editor_code_execution_tool_result, mcp_tool_result
    }

    ensures: ResponsesDroppedServerBlock.created(
        block_kind: block.kind,
        logged: true
    )

    @guidance
        -- translate_to_responses_request, the ServerToolUse / McpToolUse
        -- and the *_tool_result match arms. Each emits a tracing::debug!
        -- naming the block discriminant and id. CLAUDE.md: "capability
        -- gaps are logged, not silenced." The Anthropic provider
        -- round-trips these same blocks verbatim — switching a
        -- conversation from Claude to an OpenAI model is where the gap
        -- becomes observable, and the log is what makes it diagnosable.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules — response normalization
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Normalize a Responses API response. Two outcomes:
--   1. Billed-but-empty — no content block resulted yet OpenAI reported
--      output tokens. The assembled message was lost (a gateway
--      dropping the output array). Yields a RETRYABLE server_error
--      rather than a silently-empty agent turn the user was billed for.
--   2. Otherwise a normalized llm/LlmResponse. Output items map:
--      message -> text content (both output_text and refusal parts),
--      function_call -> tool_use, reasoning -> discarded, other ->
--      ignored. end_turn holds iff the status is "completed" AND no
--      tool call is pending.
--
-- Token accounting de-duplicates both cache detail subsets: OpenAI's
-- raw_input_tokens includes cached_tokens and cache_write_tokens, so both
-- are split into their Usage buckets and subtracted from input_tokens.
-- This keeps llm/Usage.context_window_used equal to OpenAI's own
-- input+output total instead of double-counting the cache.
rule ResponsesResponseNormalized {
    when: NormalizeResponsesResponse(raw)

    let end_turn = raw.status = "completed" and not raw.has_function_call

    ensures:
        if not raw.has_output_content and raw.raw_output_tokens > 0:
            llm/LlmError.created(
                kind: server_error,
                message: "OpenAI returned empty response with output tokens billed",
                recovery_in_progress: false,
                has_quota: false
            )
        else:
            llm/LlmResponse.created(
                end_turn: end_turn,
                usage: {
                    input_tokens: saturating_sub(raw.raw_input_tokens, raw.cached_tokens + raw.cache_write_tokens),
                    output_tokens: raw.raw_output_tokens,
                    cache_creation_tokens: raw.cache_write_tokens,
                    cache_read_tokens: raw.cached_tokens
                }
            )

    @guidance
        -- normalize_responses_api_response, called by both the
        -- non-streaming path and the streaming accumulator's
        -- into_response. The billed-but-empty branch complements the
        -- ResponsesStreamCompleted recovery fallback: the fallback
        -- salvages a message that arrived only in /response/output,
        -- this branch catches the case where even that is empty though
        -- tokens were billed. An empty response with output_tokens = 0
        -- is a genuinely-empty valid turn and takes the LlmResponse
        -- branch. GPT-5.6-era Responses usage reports cache writes in
        -- input_tokens_details.cache_write_tokens; older responses default it
        -- to zero. saturating_sub floors at 0; OpenAI guarantees the combined
        -- cache detail subsets do not exceed raw_input_tokens
        -- (ResponsesCachedSubsetOfInput), so the floor is defensive.
        -- A "reasoning" item is dropped silently (internal thinking,
        -- not transcript). A message's "refusal" part surfaces as text
        -- content — a declined turn is a real final answer, not a
        -- lost message to retry.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules — streaming accumulator
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The streaming HTTP response returned success; begin accumulating.
rule ResponsesStreamOpened {
    when: ResponsesStreamingResponseAccepted(request_id)

    ensures: ResponsesStream.created(
        status: accumulating,
        input_tokens: 0,
        output_tokens: 0,
        cached_tokens: 0,
        cache_write_tokens: 0,
        recovered_from_completed: false
    )
}

-- A response.output_text.delta event. The non-empty text delta is
-- forwarded to the UI immediately as an llm/TokenChunk.
rule ResponsesTextDeltaForwarded {
    when: ResponsesOutputTextDelta(stream, fragment)

    requires: stream.status = accumulating
    requires: fragment != ""

    ensures: ResponsesTextChunkForwarded(text: fragment)
}

-- A response.output_item.done event. The completed output item is
-- collected; its kind drives normalization.
rule ResponsesOutputItemCollected {
    when: ResponsesOutputItemDone(stream, item_kind)

    requires: stream.status = accumulating

    ensures: ResponsesOutputItem.created(stream: stream, item_kind: item_kind)
}

-- A response.completed event ends the stream successfully. Usage is
-- recorded. Fallback: if no per-item.done events were seen, the output
-- items are recovered from the terminal event's /response/output array.
rule ResponsesStreamCompleted {
    when: ResponsesCompletedEvent(stream, input_tokens, output_tokens, cached_tokens, cache_write_tokens, terminal_output)

    requires: stream.status = accumulating

    ensures:
        stream.input_tokens = input_tokens
        stream.output_tokens = output_tokens
        stream.cached_tokens = cached_tokens
        stream.cache_write_tokens = cache_write_tokens
        if stream.output_items.count = 0:
            stream.recovered_from_completed = true
            for kind in terminal_output:
                ResponsesOutputItem.created(stream: stream, item_kind: kind)
        stream.status = done

    @guidance
        -- response.completed handler. The fallback exists because a
        -- gateway path (observed 2026-05-11) emitted output_item.added
        -- and content_part.added events but no output_item.done, then
        -- response.completed carrying the full assembled message.
        -- Without recovery the accumulator finalized empty and Phoenix
        -- persisted "end_turn with empty content" despite being billed
        -- for output tokens. /response/output in the terminal event is
        -- authoritative when the per-item stream produced nothing.
        -- The `if` guard reads resulting state: output_items.count
        -- reflects items created by prior ResponsesOutputItemCollected
        -- firings, before this rule creates any.
}

-- An `error` SSE event terminates the stream. The error code is routed
-- through classification (ResponsesSseErrorClassified).
rule ResponsesStreamFailedByError {
    when: ResponsesErrorEvent(stream, code, message)

    requires: stream.status = accumulating

    ensures:
        stream.status = failed
        ClassifyResponsesError(code: code, message: message)
}

-- A response.failed event terminates the stream. Same classification
-- path as a bare `error` event — the codes are the same family.
rule ResponsesStreamFailedByResponseFailed {
    when: ResponsesResponseFailedEvent(stream, code, message)

    requires: stream.status = accumulating

    ensures:
        stream.status = failed
        ClassifyResponsesError(code: code, message: message)
}

-- A response.incomplete event terminates the stream. A content_filter
-- reason is a terminal content_filter error; any other reason is a
-- retryable server_error.
rule ResponsesStreamIncomplete {
    when: ResponsesIncompleteEvent(stream, reason)

    requires: stream.status = accumulating

    ensures:
        stream.status = failed
        if reason = "content_filter":
            llm/LlmError.created(kind: content_filter,
                message: "Response incomplete: content_filter",
                recovery_in_progress: false, has_quota: false)
        else:
            llm/LlmError.created(kind: server_error,
                message: "Response incomplete",
                recovery_in_progress: false, has_quota: false)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules — SSE error classification
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Classify a Responses API SSE error. Codex-specific terminal codes are
-- matched exactly and FIRST: usage_limit_reached and the overloaded
-- signals must not be collapsed into the generic substring buckets
-- (which would make a quota exhaustion look retryable). Anything else
-- falls through to the substring scan.
rule ResponsesSseErrorClassified {
    when: ClassifyResponsesError(code, message)

    let lower = lowercase(code)

    ensures:
        if lower = "usage_limit_reached":
            llm/LlmError.created(kind: usage_limit_reached, message: message,
                recovery_in_progress: false, has_quota: true)
        else:
            if lower = "usage_not_included":
                llm/LlmError.created(kind: auth,
                    message: "Upgrade required: this plan does not include Codex usage.",
                    recovery_in_progress: false, has_quota: false)
            else:
                if lower = "server_is_overloaded" or lower = "slow_down":
                    llm/LlmError.created(kind: server_overloaded,
                        message: "Selected model is at capacity. Try a different model.",
                        recovery_in_progress: false, has_quota: false)
                else:
                    ResponsesGenericErrorScan(code: lower, message: message)

    @guidance
        -- classify_responses_error. `lowercase` is the ASCII lowercasing
        -- the implementation applies to `code` before matching. The SSE
        -- path has no response headers, so a usage_limit_reached from a
        -- stream carries an all-None QuotaDetails — has_quota is still
        -- true (a details struct exists) and llm/QuotaBoundToUsageLimit
        -- holds. The plan-aware message formatter renders generic
        -- wording when plan_type is None.
}

-- The substring fallback for a non-codex error code. Precedence order is
-- significant — the first matching family wins — and the default is a
-- RETRYABLE server_error so the executor's retry loop engages on a code
-- Phoenix has never seen.
rule ResponsesGenericErrorClassified {
    when: ResponsesGenericErrorScan(code, message)

    ensures:
        if contains(code, "rate_limit") or contains(code, "quota") or contains(code, "requests_per"):
            llm/LlmError.created(kind: rate_limit, message: message,
                recovery_in_progress: false, has_quota: false)
        else:
            if contains(code, "auth") or contains(code, "invalid_api_key") or contains(code, "permission"):
                llm/LlmError.created(kind: auth, message: message,
                    recovery_in_progress: false, has_quota: false)
            else:
                if contains(code, "context_length") or contains(code, "token_limit") or contains(code, "max_tokens"):
                    llm/LlmError.created(kind: context_window_exceeded, message: message,
                        recovery_in_progress: false, has_quota: false)
                else:
                    if contains(code, "content_filter") or contains(code, "safety"):
                        llm/LlmError.created(kind: content_filter, message: message,
                            recovery_in_progress: false, has_quota: false)
                    else:
                        if contains(code, "invalid") or contains(code, "bad_request"):
                            llm/LlmError.created(kind: invalid_request, message: message,
                                recovery_in_progress: false, has_quota: false)
                        else:
                            llm/LlmError.created(kind: server_error, message: message,
                                recovery_in_progress: false, has_quota: false)

    @guidance
        -- classify_responses_error's precedence-ordered substring scan.
        -- `contains` is a substring test against the already-lowercased
        -- code (ResponsesSseErrorClassified lowercases it first). First
        -- match wins, in this exact order:
        --   1. rate_limit              — "rate_limit" / "quota" / "requests_per"
        --   2. auth                    — "auth" / "invalid_api_key" / "permission"
        --   3. context_window_exceeded — "context_length" / "token_limit" / "max_tokens"
        --   4. content_filter          — "content_filter" / "safety"
        --   5. invalid_request         — "invalid" / "bad_request"
        --   6. server_error            — no substring matched (retryable default)
        -- "quota" lands in branch 1 (rate_limit): only the exact code
        -- "usage_limit_reached" — matched before this rule — becomes the
        -- terminal usage_limit_reached kind. See Q2.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules — codex backend HTTP error parsing (REQ-LLM-006a)
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A codex-backend HTTP 429. Only when the body's error.type is
-- "usage_limit_reached" is this a terminal quota exhaustion — and only
-- then are the x-codex-* response headers parsed into structured
-- QuotaDetails (plan, reset time, window snapshots, credits, promo).
-- error.type "usage_not_included" is an auth/upgrade error. Any other
-- recognised-envelope 429 is a transient throttle and falls through to
-- the generic HTTP path (which yields a retryable rate_limit).
rule ResponsesCodex429Classified {
    when: CodexBackendHttpError(status, error_type?, body)

    requires: status = 429

    ensures:
        if error_type = "usage_limit_reached":
            llm/LlmError.created(kind: usage_limit_reached, message: body,
                recovery_in_progress: false, has_quota: true)
        else:
            if error_type = "usage_not_included":
                llm/LlmError.created(kind: auth,
                    message: "Upgrade required: this plan does not include Codex usage.",
                    recovery_in_progress: false, has_quota: false)
            else:
                llm/LlmError.created(kind: rate_limit, message: body,
                    recovery_in_progress: false, has_quota: false)

    @guidance
        -- parse_codex_error 429 arm. usage_limit_reached is the only
        -- branch that populates QuotaDetails from headers
        -- (parse_active_limit, parse_rate_limit_for_limit,
        -- parse_credits_snapshot, parse_promo_message) and the body's
        -- resets_at. The fall-through `rate_limit` models parse_codex_
        -- error returning None and the caller invoking from_http_status,
        -- whose 429 arm is rate_limit. This rule applies only to the
        -- codex backend; the platform Responses API never reaches it.
}

-- A codex-backend HTTP 503. The overloaded signals are a terminal
-- server_overloaded; any other 503 falls through to the generic HTTP
-- path (a retryable server_error).
rule ResponsesCodex503Classified {
    when: CodexBackendHttpError(status, error_type?, body)

    requires: status = 503

    ensures:
        if error_type = "server_is_overloaded" or error_type = "slow_down":
            llm/LlmError.created(kind: server_overloaded,
                message: "Selected model is at capacity. Try a different model.",
                recovery_in_progress: false, has_quota: false)
        else:
            llm/LlmError.created(kind: server_error, message: body,
                recovery_in_progress: false, has_quota: false)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- LOAD-BEARING. Every content part emitted into a Responses API request
-- — message content OR function_call_output — carries a discriminant the
-- API accepts. This is the formal statement of the contract the 2026-05
-- incident violated. A part with wire_type `text` or `image_url` (the
-- Chat Completions shape) fails this invariant; the API failed the
-- request with HTTP 400. The accepted set is verbatim from that 400.
invariant ResponsesContentPartTypeValid {
    for part in ResponsesWireContentParts:
        part.wire_type in {
            "input_text",
            "input_image",
            "output_text",
            "refusal",
            "input_file",
            "computer_screenshot",
            "summary_text"
        }
}

-- Tighter than the validity boundary: Phoenix only ever emits the two
-- input discriminants. Both translation rules
-- (ResponsesMessageContentTranslated, ResponsesToolResultOutputTranslated)
-- produce exactly input_text and input_image. The wider accepted set in
-- ResponsesContentPartTypeValid is what the API tolerates on input
-- (output_text / refusal / summary_text appear on the response side);
-- this invariant pins what Phoenix actually generates.
invariant ResponsesEmitsOnlyInputParts {
    for part in ResponsesWireContentParts:
        part.wire_type in { "input_text", "input_image" }
}

-- Surface-specific Rust types make cache breakpoints unrepresentable on
-- function_call_output while both types obey the same discriminant contract.
invariant ResponsesPartContractSurfaceIndependent {
    for part in ResponsesWireContentParts:
        (part.surface = function_call_output) implies
            part.wire_type in { "input_text", "input_image" }
}

-- A dropped Anthropic server block is always logged. Silent omission is
-- indistinguishable from a translator bug (CLAUDE.md). The drop is
-- intentional — the Responses API has no wire equivalent — but it must
-- leave a trace.
invariant ResponsesDroppedBlockAlwaysLogged {
    for dropped in ResponsesDroppedServerBlocks:
        dropped.logged = true
}

-- The combined cache-read and cache-write detail buckets are subsets of
-- the raw input-token count. This guarantees normalization never underflows
-- and the four llm/Usage buckets sum to OpenAI's input+output total.
invariant ResponsesCacheDetailsSubsetOfInput {
    for stream in ResponsesStreams:
        stream.status = done implies
            stream.cached_tokens + stream.cache_write_tokens <= stream.input_tokens
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------
-- All resolved 2026-05-22. Kept as prose so the file stays parseable.
--
-- Q1. RESOLVED — billed-but-empty is now a distinct outcome of
--     ResponsesResponseNormalized. A completed response with no content
--     block but raw_output_tokens > 0 yields a retryable server_error:
--     OpenAI billed for a message it did not deliver. The
--     wire-indistinguishable "genuinely empty valid turn" case
--     (output_tokens = 0) still resolves to an empty llm/LlmResponse —
--     correct, a model can finish a tool loop with nothing to add. The
--     residual case (a reasoning-only turn with output_tokens > 0 and
--     no message) takes the error branch: a degenerate turn is better
--     retried than persisted empty.
--
-- Q2. RESOLVED — keep as-is. classify_responses_error routes a code
--     *containing* "quota" to the retryable rate_limit family; only the
--     exact code "usage_limit_reached" is terminal. A hypothetical code
--     such as "insufficient_quota" would be retried rather than failing
--     terminally. Accepted: no such code is observed on the Responses
--     path today, and the path that actually matters for quota
--     exhaustion — the codex backend — signals it via the structured
--     error.type field (ResponsesCodex429Classified), not the code
--     string. The substring scan is a heuristic for the platform API's
--     less-structured errors; pre-emptively guessing an unobserved code
--     spelling would itself be a guess. Revisit if such a code appears
--     in logs.
--
-- Q3. RESOLVED — accept as a deferred capability. The reasoning output
--     item is discarded during normalization, so multi-turn reasoning
--     context is not carried between turns. The Responses API can
--     return encrypted reasoning for replay via the `include` request
--     field, but Phoenix sends an empty `include` until it has a
--     durable transcript representation for reasoning items. A known
--     limitation, not a bug; reasoning replay is separate work.
--
-- Q4. RESOLVED — scoping note, no decision needed.
--     ResponsesCodex429Classified / ResponsesCodex503Classified apply
--     only when use_codex_backend is true; the platform Responses API
--     uses ResponsesSseErrorClassified (SSE) or from_http_status (HTTP).
--     The backend selector is request-scoped configuration, modelled in
--     neither branch's trigger; both per-backend paths are specified so
--     the selector's two outcomes are each covered.
--
-- Note (stream exhausted without response.completed): if the byte
--     stream ends before a response.completed event, the accumulator
--     still assembles whatever it collected — no `done` transition, no
--     error. A degenerate path; not modelled as a rule because it
--     produces no distinct observable outcome beyond a possibly-empty
--     response, which the billed-but-empty guard (Q1) now screens.
